    ElectroFit <span class="tag">Body</span>
  </span>
  <span class="nav-meta">$50M Model · Structure · Internal</span>
</nav>

<div class="wrap">

  <header class="cover reveal">
    <div class="eyebrow">$50M Series A · Financial Model · Confidential</div>
    <h1>The $50M, structured to <span class="it">clear the committee.</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">A bigger raise the review can actually underwrite: capital released in milestone-gated tranches, spent on what builds the moat, modeled on assumptions benchmarked against real comparables — and a return profile that holds even when the base case is cut.</p>
    <div class="meta">
      <div><div class="k">Raise</div><div class="v cy">$50M</div></div>
      <div><div class="k">Structure</div><div class="v">3 tranches</div></div>
      <div><div class="k">Break-even</div><div class="v gr">Year 3</div></div>
      <div><div class="k">Base return</div><div class="v">~14.5×</div></div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="callout reveal">
    <div class="cl">What this model fixes</div>
    <p>It exists to answer the two objections that would sink the round: that the <strong>entry price assumed traction the company doesn't have</strong> (R2), and that the <strong>projections strained credibility</strong> (R6). It answers both structurally — tranched capital so the investor's money de-risks as milestones are hit, spend weighted to defensibility rather than marketing, assumptions deliberately set below the prior plan and benchmarked, and a no-bridge path to profit.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2 class="sec-title">The raise — three <span class="it">milestone-gated</span> tranches.</h2></div>
    <p>The single change that makes $50M underwritable pre-product: the investor doesn't deploy it all on day one. Capital releases in three tranches, each unlocked by a milestone that retires real risk. The lead's first dollars enter at an early-stage price; later dollars enter at step-ups the company's own progress has justified. The investor is never underwriting the whole journey at once — and the founders aren't priced as if the journey is already done.</p>
    <div class="tranches">
      <div class="tr t1">
        <div class="tr-n">Tranche 1 · at close</div>
        <div class="tr-amt">$20M</div>
        <div class="tr-val">prices round · ~$90M post</div>
        <div class="tr-gate">Unlocks: initial close</div>
        <div class="tr-funds">Founding team (CTO, regulatory, senior eng), FDA 510(k) filing, first 6,000-unit production run, ARC platform v1 + iPad-locked experience, launch GTM.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tr t2">
        <div class="tr-n">Tranche 2 · ~month 12–15</div>
        <div class="tr-amt">$18M</div>
        <div class="tr-val">step-up · performance-priced</div>
        <div class="tr-gate">Unlocks on</div>
        <ul>
          <li>FDA 510(k) clearance secured</li>
          <li>6,000 units shipped</li>
          <li>Subscription attach ≥ 50%</li>
          <li>Second supplier qualified</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="tr t3">
        <div class="tr-n">Tranche 3 · ~month 24–27</div>
        <div class="tr-amt">$12M</div>
        <div class="tr-val">step-up · performance-priced</div>
        <div class="tr-gate">Unlocks on</div>
        <ul>
          <li>$40M+ revenue run-rate</li>
          <li>13,000+ active subscribers</li>
          <li>Gross margin ≥ 44%</li>
          <li>EBITDA tracking to plan</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Tranche valuations are illustrative and negotiated with the lead; the structural point is the gating, not the exact step-ups. A committed lead funds the full $50M; release is milestone-contingent.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2 class="sec-title">Use of funds — capital that <span class="it">buys defensibility.</span></h2></div>
    <p>The review's sharpest framing was that ElectroFit risked looking like "a marketing company white-labeling someone else's product." The allocation answers it directly: <strong>the majority of the raise goes to engineering, the platform, hardware, and regulatory — the things that compound into a moat — not into paid acquisition.</strong></p>
    <div class="uof">
      <div class="uof-row def"><div class="lbl">Engineering &amp; Platform<small>ARC AI · iPad platform · controller roadmap · data infra</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:68%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$17.0M</div></div>
      <div class="uof-row gtm"><div class="lbl">Go-to-Market<small>paid · influencer · content · brand</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:44%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$11.0M</div></div>
      <div class="uof-row def"><div class="lbl">Hardware &amp; Supply Chain<small>production · custom controller NRE · dual-sourcing</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:32%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$8.0M</div></div>
      <div class="uof-row gtm"><div class="lbl">Operations &amp; Fulfillment<small>warehousing · support · returns</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:20%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$5.0M</div></div>
      <div class="uof-row def"><div class="lbl">Regulatory &amp; Clinical<small>510(k) · biocompatibility · clinical evidence · QMS</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:16%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$4.0M</div></div>
      <div class="uof-row gtm"><div class="lbl">Team &amp; G&amp;A<small>exec hires · finance · legal · corporate</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:14%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$3.5M</div></div>
      <div class="uof-row gtm"><div class="lbl">Working Capital &amp; Reserve<small>buffer · contingency</small></div><div class="uof-bar"><i style="width:6%"></i></div><div class="uof-amt">$1.5M</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout"><div class="cl">The signal</div><p><strong>58% — $29M of the $50M — goes to engineering, hardware, and regulatory.</strong> Go-to-market is 22%. That ratio is the proof, in dollars, that this is a platform-and-hardware company building durable assets, not a brand buying growth it can't keep.</p></div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2 class="sec-title">The base case — <span class="it">five-year</span> P&amp;L.</h2></div>
    <p>The same trajectory as the roadmap, shown as the numbers behind it. Two years of disciplined investment, profitability in Year 3, and compounding margin after.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th style="text-align:right">Y1</th><th style="text-align:right">Y2</th><th style="text-align:right">Y3</th><th style="text-align:right">Y4</th><th style="text-align:right">Y5</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Units sold (annual)</td><td class="num">6,000</td><td class="num">18,000</td><td class="num">45,000</td><td class="num">85,000</td><td class="num">140,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Active subscriptions</td><td class="num">3,600</td><td class="num">13,000</td><td class="num">38,000</td><td class="num">85,000</td><td class="num">165,000</td></tr>
        <tr class="hl"><td><strong>Revenue</strong></td><td class="num cy">$13M</td><td class="num cy">$48M</td><td class="num cy">$130M</td><td class="num cy">$285M</td><td class="num cy">$520M</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gross margin</td><td class="num">38%</td><td class="num">44%</td><td class="num">50%</td><td class="num">55%</td><td class="num">60%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>EBITDA</strong></td><td class="num am">−$16M</td><td class="num am">−$6M</td><td class="num gr">+$14M</td><td class="num gr">+$58M</td><td class="num gr">+$165M</td></tr>
        <tr class="tot"><td>Cumulative EBITDA</td><td class="num am">−$16M</td><td class="num am">−$22M</td><td class="num am">−$8M</td><td class="num gr">+$50M</td><td class="num gr">+$215M</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="callout"><div class="cl">The no-bridge proof</div><p>Peak cumulative operating deficit is <strong>−$22M, reached at the end of Year 2</strong> — against $50M raised. The company turns the corner with roughly $28M of buffer it never needs to deploy as burn, and self-funds all growth from Year 3. <strong>There is no second raise in this plan.</strong> The Series A is the last dollar of primary capital the business requires.</p></div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2 class="sec-title">The revenue build — <span class="it">hardware is the wedge.</span></h2></div>
    <p>Where the money actually comes from — and the most important line in the model: <strong>hardware falls from 88% of revenue to 51% as the recurring and platform streams compound.</strong> By Year 5, nearly half the business is subscription, marketplace, and ancillary — software-grade, high-margin, and far more defensible than a garment. This is the moat reframe made numerical.</p>
    <div class="rev">
      <div class="rev-legend">
        <span><i class="s-hw"></i> Hardware</span>
        <span><i class="s-sub"></i> Subscription</span>
        <span><i class="s-mkt"></i> Marketplace</span>
        <span><i class="s-supp"></i> Supplements / apparel</span>
        <span><i class="s-cert"></i> Certification / B2B</span>
      </div>
      <div class="rev-row"><div class="rev-yr">YEAR 1</div><div class="rev-bar"><i class="s-hw" style="width:87.7%"></i><i class="s-sub" style="width:7.7%"></i><i class="s-mkt" style="width:2.3%"></i><i class="s-supp" style="width:1.5%"></i><i class="s-cert" style="width:0.8%"></i></div><div class="rev-tot">$13M</div></div>
      <div class="rev-row"><div class="rev-yr">YEAR 2</div><div class="rev-bar"><i class="s-hw" style="width:71.3%"></i><i class="s-sub" style="width:14.6%"></i><i class="s-mkt" style="width:6.3%"></i><i class="s-supp" style="width:6.3%"></i><i class="s-cert" style="width:1.7%"></i></div><div class="rev-tot">$48M</div></div>
      <div class="rev-row"><div class="rev-yr">YEAR 3</div><div class="rev-bar"><i class="s-hw" style="width:65.8%"></i><i class="s-sub" style="width:18.5%"></i><i class="s-mkt" style="width:8.5%"></i><i class="s-supp" style="width:5.4%"></i><i class="s-cert" style="width:1.9%"></i></div><div class="rev-tot">$130M</div></div>
      <div class="rev-row"><div class="rev-yr">YEAR 4</div><div class="rev-bar"><i class="s-hw" style="width:56.7%"></i><i class="s-sub" style="width:19.3%"></i><i class="s-mkt" style="width:12.3%"></i><i class="s-supp" style="width:9.8%"></i><i class="s-cert" style="width:1.9%"></i></div><div class="rev-tot">$285M</div></div>
      <div class="rev-row"><div class="rev-yr">YEAR 5</div><div class="rev-bar"><i class="s-hw" style="width:51.2%"></i><i class="s-sub" style="width:16.9%"></i><i class="s-mkt" style="width:13.1%"></i><i class="s-supp" style="width:11.9%"></i><i class="s-cert" style="width:6.9%"></i></div><div class="rev-tot">$520M</div></div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Recurring + platform revenue (everything but hardware) grows from ~$1.6M to ~$254M — from 12% to 49% of the business. That mix shift is what justifies a software-style exit multiple rather than a hardware one.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2 class="sec-title">Unit economics — <span class="it">conservative on purpose.</span></h2></div>
    <p>The review flagged the prior model's numbers as "best-case-on-best-case." So every assumption here is set at or below the optimistic edge and tied to a real benchmark. Most visibly: the <strong>LTV/CAC has been cut from 13.6× to ~8×</strong> — not because the business is worse, but because a credible 8× builds more trust than an incredible 13.6×.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Assumption</th><th>This model</th><th>Basis / benchmark</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Retail price</strong></td><td class="num">$1,900</td><td>Below Peloton Bike+ ($2,495) and Tonal ($3,495); above entry wearables</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hardware GM</strong></td><td class="num">38% → 60%</td><td>OEM cost decline + proprietary controller program (Yr 3+)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Subscription ARPU</strong></td><td class="num">$41.50</td><td>Blended: Tier 1 $34.99 / Tier 2 $47.99 (ARC), ~70% choose Tier 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Subscription attach</strong></td><td class="num">60%</td><td>First 6 months bundled at point of sale; in line with connected-fitness attach</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Monthly churn</strong></td><td class="num">~4%</td><td>Connected-fitness range is 3–7%; modeled toward — not beyond — the strong end</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Blended CAC</strong></td><td class="num">~$350</td><td>Premium-hardware DTC $300–600; founder organic + affiliate compress it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>3-yr LTV / customer</strong></td><td class="num">~$2,800</td><td>HW gross profit + subscription + marketplace + ancillary, conservatively retained</td></tr>
        <tr class="hl"><td><strong>LTV / CAC</strong></td><td class="num cy">~8×</td><td>Deliberately below prior 13.6×; vs. Peloton ~6× peak, Tonal ~4×</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <div class="statgrid">
      <div><div class="sv cy">~8×</div><div class="sk">LTV / CAC (base)</div></div>
      <div><div class="sv">~$2,800</div><div class="sk">3-yr LTV / customer</div></div>
      <div><div class="sv">60%</div><div class="sk">Attach at point of sale</div></div>
      <div><div class="sv">~4%</div><div class="sk">Monthly churn (modeled)</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2 class="sec-title">The returns <span class="it">bridge.</span></h2></div>
    <p>Capital in: <strong>$50M</strong>, deployed across the three tranches. Because the plan is self-funding from Year 3 and requires no further primary round, the Series A position is <strong>not diluted by future financings</strong> — only by ordinary option-pool refreshes, leaving the lead group at roughly <strong>25% at exit</strong>. Exit framed on Year 5 ($520M revenue, $165M EBITDA), against healthy consumer-health / wearable comps.</p>
    <div class="rets">
      <div class="ret cons">
        <div class="ret-lbl">Conservative</div>
        <div class="ret-mult">~10×</div>
        <div class="ret-sub"><b>$2.0B exit</b><br>3.8× revenue · 12× EBITDA</div>
      </div>
      <div class="ret base">
        <div class="ret-lbl">Base</div>
        <div class="ret-mult">~14.5×</div>
        <div class="ret-sub"><b>$2.9B exit</b><br>5.6× revenue · 17.6× EBITDA</div>
      </div>
      <div class="ret up">
        <div class="ret-lbl">Upside</div>
        <div class="ret-mult">~22×</div>
        <div class="ret-sub"><b>$4.5B exit</b><br>IPO-grade · 8.7× revenue</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>And the number that matters most to a risk-focused committee — the downside: <strong>even at 70% of the base case</strong>, a haircut that assumes meaningful underperformance, the position still returns roughly <strong>7–9×</strong>. The asymmetry is the point. The upside is venture-scale; the downside, on a plan that reaches profitability without a bridge, is still a strong multiple.</p>
    <div class="pull">"Tranched entry, defensibility-weighted spend, benchmarked assumptions, no bridge. The same company — re-presented as one a committee can actually say yes to."</div>
  </section>

  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="sec reveal">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">07</span><h2 class="sec-title">Why a committee can <span class="it">underwrite this.</span></h2></div>
    <p>Set against the review's two financial objections, the model now has an answer to each:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>The objection</th><th>The answer in this model</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>R2 — entry price assumed traction</strong></td><td>Capital is tranched and milestone-gated; the first dollars enter at a defensible ~$90M post, later dollars at performance-priced step-ups. The investor underwrites one stage at a time.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>R6 — projections strained credibility</strong></td><td>Every assumption is benchmarked and set below the prior plan; LTV/CAC cut to ~8×; the revenue build reconciles stream-by-stream; downside still returns ~7–9×.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Moat / "marketing company"</strong></td><td>58% of the raise funds engineering, hardware, and regulatory; revenue shifts from 88% hardware to 49% recurring/platform.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Capital efficiency / runway</strong></td><td>Peak burn of $22M inside a $50M raise; profitable in Year 3; no bridge round in the plan.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>None of this changes the company. It changes whether the company is <strong>fundable</strong> — and paired with the team and board from the governance plan, it turns a deal a committee would pass on into one it can defend.</p>
